\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances the symbolic execution of the goal's {eventually} statement across one
inlined Montgomery-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current state
variable by a fresh successor state and recording the squaring's specification
as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which occurrence/variant of the
subroutine block to step over. The second, a {string list} of two names, gives
first the variable naming the result value written by the block, and second the
input value being squared. The conclusion's {eventually} body is unchanged but
re-stated about a new, later state variable, and the program-counter assumption
is updated to the address following the executed block. Every assumption
mentioning the old state — register contents, {bytes_loaded}, memory-content
equations, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to
the current one — is discharged and replaced by the corresponding assumption
about the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the memory
component this block writes. Two assumptions are added: a memory-content
equation for the destination buffer holding the freshly named result, and a
conditional arithmetic assumption bounding the input and equating the result
with the Montgomery square of the input modulo the prime. Exactly one subgoal
results.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of step, applied to goals in
Montgomery point-arithmetic symbolic-execution states.

\ENDDOC
